\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Exact elimination of the neutral bit}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{For the \vocabk{extended orbit weight}{fdip-0121}, summing the
neutral bit replaces each site factor by the sum of its two bit
weights. Explicitly, for every connection and every scalar
configuration,
##{\sum_{\zeta\in\{0,1\}^X}\Xi(U,\phi,\zeta)
 =\Xi_c(U,\phi),}
where #{\Xi_c} is the weight with the same face and edge factors
and with site factors
##{c_x(s)=\alpha_{0,x}(s)+\alpha_{1,x}(s).}
Each #{c_x} is constant on every orbit. Its value on the orbit
of #{\phi^\circ_i} is
#{\alpha_{0,x}(\phi^\circ_i)+\alpha_{1,x}(\phi^\circ_i)}, so two
orbits may carry different constants. For an observable
#{f(U,\phi)} with values in a #{k}-module and independent of
#{\zeta},
##{\sum_{U,\phi,\zeta}\Xi(U,\phi,\zeta)\,f(U,\phi)
 =\sum_{U,\phi}\Xi_c(U,\phi)\,f(U,\phi).}
The two sides are equal as elements of the module. When their common
sum for #{f=1} is a unit of #{k}, the two normalized values of
#{f} are equal.}

\proof{
\p{The face and edge products do not depend on #{\zeta}. The site
product is #{\prod_x\alpha_{\zeta_x,x}(\phi_x)}. Summing a finite
product of independent two-point choices factors as a product of
sums, which is the displayed formula for #{c_x}. Invariance of each
bit weight gives #{c_x(gs)=c_x(s)}, so #{c_x} is constant on each
orbit. Two orbits may receive different constants: on
#{\mathcal O_i} and #{\mathcal O_j} the values are the sums of the
two bit weights at the chosen reference points of those orbits.
Multiplying by #{f(U,\phi)} and summing over #{U} and #{\phi}
preserves the equality. If the common sum #{Z} is a unit, both
normalized values equal #{Z^{-1}} times the displayed module element.}
}
